\title{Superposition over Non-Archimedean Value Fields:\\
       The Standard Part as a Measurement Rule}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{2026-07-02}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
We study finite superpositions whose amplitudes are drawn from a non-Archimedean
ordered field --- an ordered field extending the real numbers and containing
nonzero infinitesimals, the setting exemplified by Conway's surreal numbers.
Assigning to each branch its Born weight $w_i = \alpha_i^2 / \sum_j \alpha_j^2$
produces a family of weights summing to $1$ \emph{exactly} in the field, but
whose individual entries may be infinitesimal. We propose the \emph{standard
part} of the Born weight, $p_i = \st(w_i)$, as the rule governing what an
observer records, and prove that it constitutes a coherent theory of measurement.
Under the natural hypotheses that all amplitudes are limited and the total weight
is appreciable, the observed probabilities are nonnegative, sum to $1$, are
additive over disjoint families of branches, and assign probability exactly $0$
to any branch of infinitesimal amplitude. Thus a branch may carry a strictly
positive weight in the exact ledger while being observationally invisible. We show
the same collapse arises in a purely classical lexicographic-probability model,
where the standard part reduces to projection onto the leading layer, and we
outline a hierarchy of visibility levels indexed by infinitesimal order.
\end{abstract}

\maketitle

\section{Introduction}

The Born rule assigns to a superposition $|\psi\rangle = \sum_i \alpha_i |s_i\rangle$
the outcome probabilities $p_i \propto \alpha_i^2$. Classically the amplitudes are
real (or complex) and the normalization $\sum_i p_i = 1$ is an ordinary real
identity. This paper asks what happens when the amplitudes range over a strictly
larger ordered field containing genuine infinitesimals. Such fields arise
naturally: Conway's surreal numbers form the largest ordered field and contain,
alongside every real number, an entire scale of infinities and infinitesimals; the
hyperreals of nonstandard analysis and the Levi-Civita field provide equally
serviceable homes for the infinitely small.

The motivating puzzle is the status of an \emph{infinitesimally weighted branch}.
In the exact arithmetic of the field, such a branch has a positive, nonzero
weight, and total probability is conserved exactly. Yet no finite experiment can
distinguish an infinitesimal chance from zero. We resolve the tension with the
\emph{standard part} map, the canonical ring homomorphism sending each limited
field element to the unique real number infinitesimally close to it. Applying it
to the Born weights yields observed probabilities that reproduce ordinary
probability theory on the visible branches and annihilate every infinitesimal
branch, all while the underlying exact normalization is preserved. Infinitesimal
possibilities are thus \emph{real in the ledger and invisible in the world}.

\section{Non-Archimedean value fields and the standard part}

Throughout, $\FF$ denotes an ordered field with $\RR \subseteq \FF$ as an ordered
subfield, and $\FF$ is \emph{non-Archimedean}: there exists $\eps \in \FF$ with
$0 < \eps < 1/n$ for every positive integer $n$. Any such $\eps$ is an
\emph{infinitesimal}. Concretely $\FF$ may be taken to be the surreal numbers, a
hyperreal field, or the Levi-Civita field.

\begin{definition}[Magnitude classes]
For $x \in \FF$: $x$ is \emph{limited} if $|x| \le n$ for some positive integer
$n$; \emph{infinitesimal} if $|x| < 1/n$ for every positive integer $n$;
\emph{appreciable} if limited but not infinitesimal.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Magnitude arithmetic]\label{lem:mag}
\begin{enumerate}
\item A sum or product of limited elements is limited.
\item A product of an infinitesimal and a limited element is infinitesimal.
\item If $a$ is infinitesimal and $b$ is appreciable, then $a/b$ is infinitesimal.
\item The square of an infinitesimal is infinitesimal; the square of an
appreciable element is appreciable.
\end{enumerate}
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
(1)--(2) follow from the defining inequalities. For (3), $b$ appreciable makes
$1/b$ limited, so $a/b = a\cdot(1/b)$ is infinitesimal by (2). For (4), $|a|\le r$
for all real $r>0$ gives $|a|^2 \le r^2$ for all such $r$; if $b$ is appreciable
then $c \le |b| \le n$ for some real $c>0$ and integer $n$, so
$c^2 \le b^2 \le n^2$.
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}[Standard part]
The \emph{standard part} is the map $\st : L \to \RR$ on the ring $L$ of limited
elements, sending a limited $x$ to the unique real $r$ with $x - r$ infinitesimal.
\end{definition}

\begin{proposition}[Properties of the standard part]\label{prop:st}
The map $\st$ is a surjective ordered ring homomorphism:
\begin{enumerate}
\item $\st(x+y) = \st(x)+\st(y)$ and $\st(xy)=\st(x)\st(y)$ for $x,y \in L$;
\item $\st(r)=r$ for every real $r$; in particular $\st(1)=1$;
\item $x \le y \implies \st(x) \le \st(y)$;
\item for limited $x$, $\st(x)=0$ iff $x$ is infinitesimal; $\ker \st$ is the
ideal of infinitesimals.
\end{enumerate}
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
Writing $x = \st(x)+\iota_x$, $y=\st(y)+\iota_y$ with $\iota_x,\iota_y$
infinitesimal, the sum and product identities follow from
Lemma~\ref{lem:mag}, since the error terms are infinitesimal. Order preservation
holds because a real strictly below $\st(x)$ stays below $x$. Statement (4) is the
definition of infinitesimal.
\end{proof}

\section{Superpositions over a value field}

\begin{definition}[Superposition]
A \emph{superposition over $\FF$} is a finite indexed family
$|\psi\rangle = ((\alpha_i, s_i))_{i=1}^n$ with \emph{amplitudes} $\alpha_i \in
\FF$ and distinct \emph{branch labels} $s_i$, written
$|\psi\rangle = \sum_{i=1}^n \alpha_i |s_i\rangle$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Total weight, Born weights, admissibility]
The \emph{total weight} is $Z(\psi) = \sum_{i=1}^n \alpha_i^2 \in \FF$. When
$Z(\psi)\ne 0$, the \emph{Born weight} of branch $i$ is $w_i = \alpha_i^2/Z(\psi)$.
The superposition is \emph{admissible} if every $\alpha_i$ is limited and
$Z(\psi)$ is appreciable.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Observation functional]
For an admissible superposition, the \emph{observed probability} of branch $i$ is
$p_i = \st(w_i)$, and for $A \subseteq \{1,\dots,n\}$ the \emph{observation
functional} is $P(A) = \sum_{i\in A} p_i = \st\big(\sum_{i\in A} w_i\big)$.
\end{definition}

\section{Core theorems}

Fix an admissible superposition with total weight $Z$ and Born weights $w_i$.

\begin{theorem}[Exact normalization]\label{thm:exact}
In $\FF$, $\ \sum_{i=1}^n w_i = 1.$
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
$\sum_i w_i = (\sum_i \alpha_i^2)/Z = Z/Z = 1$, valid since $Z \ne 0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Nonnegativity and standard normalization]\label{thm:norm}
$p_i \ge 0$ for all $i$, and $\sum_{i=1}^n p_i = 1$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Each $w_i = \alpha_i^2/Z \ge 0$: the numerator is a square and $Z>0$ (an
appreciable, hence nonzero, sum of squares). Order preservation of $\st$ gives
$p_i \ge 0$. Additivity of $\st$ with Theorem~\ref{thm:exact} gives
$\sum_i p_i = \st(\sum_i w_i) = \st(1) = 1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Unobservability of infinitesimal branches]\label{thm:unobs}
If $\alpha_k$ is infinitesimal, then $p_k = 0$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By Lemma~\ref{lem:mag}(4), $\alpha_k^2$ is infinitesimal; since $Z$ is
appreciable, Lemma~\ref{lem:mag}(3) makes $w_k = \alpha_k^2/Z$ infinitesimal, so
$p_k = \st(w_k) = 0$ by Proposition~\ref{prop:st}(4).
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Positive weight, zero observation]
If $\alpha_k \ne 0$ is infinitesimal, then $w_k > 0$ in $\FF$ while $p_k = 0$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[Finite additivity]\label{thm:add}
For disjoint $A,B$, $\ P(A\cup B) = P(A)+P(B)$, with $P(\varnothing)=0$ and
$P(\{1,\dots,n\})=1$. Hence $P$ is a nonnegative, normalized, finitely additive
probability measure on the finite branch algebra.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Immediate from additivity of finite sums together with
Theorem~\ref{thm:norm}.
\end{proof}

\section{A worked example}

Let $\eps>0$ be infinitesimal and consider
\[
|\psi\rangle = \tfrac{1}{\sqrt{2}}|0\rangle + \tfrac{1}{\sqrt{2}}|1\rangle
             + \tfrac{1}{\sqrt{2}}\eps\,|\eps\rangle .
\]
The amplitudes $\tfrac{1}{\sqrt2}, \tfrac{1}{\sqrt2}$ are appreciable and
$\tfrac{1}{\sqrt2}\eps$ is infinitesimal, all limited. The total weight is
$Z = 1 + \tfrac12\eps^2$, appreciable, so $|\psi\rangle$ is admissible. The Born
weights $w_0 = w_1 = \tfrac{1/2}{1+\frac12\eps^2}$ and
$w_\eps = \tfrac{\frac12\eps^2}{1+\frac12\eps^2}$ sum to $1$ exactly. Since
$\st(1+\tfrac12\eps^2)=1$,
\[
p_0 = p_1 = \tfrac12, \qquad p_\eps = 0 .
\]
The infinitesimal branch has strictly positive exact weight yet zero observed
probability, and $p_0+p_1+p_\eps = 1$.

\section{The classical mirror: lexicographic probability}

\begin{definition}[Lexicographic probability system]
A \emph{lexicographic probability system} of depth $d$ assigns to each outcome
$i$ a vector $\mathbf{q}_i = (q_i^{(0)},\dots,q_i^{(d-1)}) \in \RR_{\ge 0}^d$ whose
level-$\ell$ marginals $\sum_i q_i^{(\ell)}$ each equal $1$; outcomes are compared
lexicographically.
\end{definition}

Encode such a system by $Q_i = \sum_{\ell=0}^{d-1} q_i^{(\ell)} \eps^\ell \in \FF$.

\begin{proposition}[Lexicographic collapse]
$\sum_j Q_j = 1 + (\text{infinitesimal})$ is appreciable, each $Q_i$ is limited,
and $\st\big(Q_i / \sum_j Q_j\big) = q_i^{(0)}$. An outcome with $q_i^{(0)}=0$ but
$q_i^{(1)}>0$ has observed probability $0$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
$\sum_j Q_j = 1 + (\text{infinitesimal})$ since the level-$0$ marginal is $1$,
hence has standard part $1$; $\st(Q_i)=q_i^{(0)}$; multiplicativity of $\st$ gives
the quotient $q_i^{(0)}/1$.
\end{proof}

Thus the standard part is projection onto the primary layer of a lexicographic
system, and Theorem~\ref{thm:unobs} and this proposition are one theorem viewed
through two windows.

\section{Algorithms}

When $\FF$ is represented by truncated $\eps$-expansions, the framework is
computable. \emph{Algorithm A} (observed distribution): square each amplitude, sum
to obtain $Z$, verify $Z$ appreciable (order-$0$ term nonzero), divide by $Z$
using the series inverse, and take order-$0$ coefficients; this runs in
$O(n d^2)$ for $n$ branches and truncation depth $d$, and its output sums to $1$
by Theorem~\ref{thm:norm}. \emph{Algorithm B} (visibility level): compute the
valuation of $w_k$ --- the least power of $\eps$ with nonzero coefficient --- which
gives the level at which branch $k$ first becomes visible.

\section{Extensions and future directions}

\textbf{Finitely additive measure.} Theorem~\ref{thm:add} establishes finite
additivity on the branch algebra; the natural closure extends this to countably
many branches, using that $\st$ transports the exact normalization identity term
by term. \textbf{Scaling invariance.} Observability should be invariant under
multiplication of amplitudes by an appreciable factor, since it depends only on
order of magnitude relative to the total weight. \textbf{Visibility hierarchy.}
Refining the field with higher-order infinitesimals yields observation functionals
$\st_0, \st_1, \dots$ reading successive coefficients of $\eps$; a branch of
weight $\sim \eps^k$ is invisible to $\st_0,\dots,\st_{k-1}$ but visible to
$\st_k$, producing a filtration of the state space by hidden depth, realized
explicitly by the lexicographic model.

\section{Conclusion}

When the amplitudes of a finite superposition are drawn from a non-Archimedean
ordered field, the standard part of the Born weights is the correct measurement
rule: it preserves total probability exactly, respects nonnegativity and finite
additivity, and renders infinitesimal branches observationally invisible. An
explicit three-branch state and a classical lexicographic model illustrate the
phenomenon, and the outlined extensions chart a path toward a full
non-Archimedean measurement theory.

\end{document}

